{"content":"The regression was in `sqlparse/filters/reindent.py`'s `_get_offset` method. It computed the current line via `raw.splitlines()[-2]`, but should use `[-1]` (the last/current line). When `raw` had no newline yet (e.g., token on the first line of a statement, as happens with parenthesis groups, WHERE clauses, identifier lists, CASE, VALUES, etc.), `splitlines()` returned a single-element list, and `[-2]` raised `IndexError: list index out of range`. This matches the reported crash and explains why so many reindent-related formatting tests failed.

Fix applied: changed `line = (raw or '\n').splitlines()[-2]` to `line = (raw or '\n').splitlines()[-1]`.

Verified with multiple manual tests covering parenthesis, WHERE/AND, plain SELECT lists, CASE expressions, and VALUES clauses — all now format correctly without errors, matching expected sqlparse reindent behavior.","tool_calls":[{"id":"final_1","name":"finish","arguments":{"summary":"Fixed regression in sqlparse/filters/reindent.py: ReindentFilter._get_offset used raw.splitlines()[-2] which raised IndexError when the accumulated raw text before a token had no newline yet (single-line splitlines result), e.g. for parenthesis, WHERE, identifier list, CASE, VALUES processing. Changed index to [-1] to correctly reference the current/last line. Verified via manual sqlparse.format(..., reindent=True) calls covering parenthesis grouping, WHERE/AND clauses, plain column lists, CASE expressions, and VALUES clauses — all produce correct output with no errors."}}]}
